    "updated_at": "2017-01-25T19:57:30Z",
    "body": "The changes look pretty reasonable, for the most part.  One thing that's not clear to me is what the actual type declaration for the callback function being created looks like.  I assume it should be an `EventHandlerNonNull` or an `OnBeforeUnloadEventHandlerNonNull` or an `OnErrorEventHandlerNonNull` depending on some conditions, right?  That's not obvious, even with the new text.\r\n\r\nIn terms of tests...  Let's think about how the incumbent global would actually be observable.  First of all, our callback here is backed by an actual JS function, and a non-builtin one at that.  So any calls made inside that function would have that function's global as incumbent anyway.  This is a viable option for testing which global that function was created in, but that can also be tested directly by getting the relevant `.onfoo` and using instanceof, right?\r\n\r\nBut I think we should be able to observe the incumbent global attached to the callback via return value conversions.  `EventHandlerNonNull` and `OnErrorEventHandlerNonNull` are defined to return \"any\", which doesn't do any interesting conversion steps.  However `OnBeforeUnloadEventHandlerNonNull` has a \"DOMString?\" return value.  So we might be able to test things here with an onbeforeunload handler whose return value is a JS object whose `toString` property is some bound built-in function whose behavior depends on the incumbent global, right?  Or for that matter whose `toString` _getter_ is a bound built-in function, etc.\r\n\r\nTesting entry globals is easier, obviously.  Just do something in the handler function that relies on the entry global.",
